WEBVTT

00:00.660 --> 00:03.140
Find the slope of the line
that goes through the ordered

00:03.140 --> 00:07.600
pairs 7, negative 1 and
negative 3, negative 1.

00:07.600 --> 00:09.900
Let me just do a
quick graph of these

00:09.900 --> 00:12.830
just so we can visualize
what they look like.

00:12.830 --> 00:19.180
So let me draw a
quick graph over here.

00:19.180 --> 00:22.200
So our first point
is 7, negative 1.

00:22.200 --> 00:25.330
So 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7.

00:25.330 --> 00:27.030
This is the x-axis.

00:27.030 --> 00:28.640
7, negative 1.

00:28.640 --> 00:31.300
So it's 7, negative 1
is right over there.

00:31.300 --> 00:33.800
7, negative 1.

00:33.800 --> 00:36.070
This, of course, is the y-axis.

00:36.070 --> 00:39.680
And then the next point
is negative 3, negative 1.

00:39.680 --> 00:42.710
So we go back 3 in the
horizontal direction.

00:42.710 --> 00:49.140
Negative 3 for the y-coordinate
is still negative 1.

00:49.140 --> 00:53.600
So the line that connects these
two points will look like this.

00:53.600 --> 00:56.830
It will look like that.

00:56.830 --> 00:59.370
Now, they're asking us to find
the slope of the line that

00:59.370 --> 01:00.661
goes through the ordered pairs.

01:00.661 --> 01:01.890
Find the slope of this line.

01:01.890 --> 01:03.890
And just to give a little
bit of intuition here,

01:03.890 --> 01:06.980
slope is a measure of
a line's inclination.

01:06.980 --> 01:09.170
And the way that
it's defined-- slope

01:09.170 --> 01:18.610
is defined as rise over run, or
change in y over change in x,

01:18.610 --> 01:24.630
or sometimes you'll see it
defined as the variable m.

01:24.630 --> 01:26.800
And then they'll
define change in y

01:26.800 --> 01:29.640
as just being the
second y-coordinate

01:29.640 --> 01:31.750
minus the first
y-coordinate and then

01:31.750 --> 01:34.430
the change in x as the
second x-coordinate

01:34.430 --> 01:35.980
minus the first x-coordinate.

01:35.980 --> 01:37.814
These are all different
variations in slope,

01:37.814 --> 01:39.563
but hopefully you'll
appreciate that these

01:39.563 --> 01:40.800
are measuring inclination.

01:40.800 --> 01:43.530
If I rise a ton when
I run a little bit,

01:43.530 --> 01:46.800
if I move a little bit in the x
direction, and I rise a bunch,

01:46.800 --> 01:48.360
then I have a very steep line.

01:48.360 --> 01:50.510
I have a very steep
upward-sloping line.

01:50.510 --> 01:54.190
If I don't change at
all when I run a bit,

01:54.190 --> 01:55.630
then I have a very low slope.

01:55.630 --> 01:57.380
And that's actually
what's happening here.

01:57.380 --> 01:59.910
I'm going from-- you
could either view

01:59.910 --> 02:01.470
this as the starting
point or view

02:01.470 --> 02:02.600
this as the starting point.

02:02.600 --> 02:04.350
But let's view this
as the starting point.

02:04.350 --> 02:06.450
So this negative 3, 1.

02:06.450 --> 02:13.340
If I go from negative 3,
negative 1 to 7, negative 1,

02:13.340 --> 02:14.820
I'm running a good bit.

02:14.820 --> 02:16.190
I'm going from negative 3.

02:16.190 --> 02:19.630
My x value is negative 3 here,
and it goes all the way to 7.

02:19.630 --> 02:24.250
So my change in x here is 10.

02:24.250 --> 02:27.890
To go from negative 3 to 7,
I changed my x value by 10.

02:27.890 --> 02:29.710
But what's my change in y?

02:29.710 --> 02:32.490
Well, my y value
here is negative 1,

02:32.490 --> 02:35.030
and my y value over here
is still negative 1.

02:35.030 --> 02:37.950
So my change in y is a 0.

02:37.950 --> 02:40.570
My change in y is going to be 0.

02:40.570 --> 02:42.910
My y value does not
change no matter

02:42.910 --> 02:45.380
how much I change my x value.

02:45.380 --> 02:49.910
So the slope here
is going to be--

02:49.910 --> 02:54.040
when we run 10,
what was our rise?

02:54.040 --> 02:55.840
How much did we change in y?

02:55.840 --> 02:57.140
Well, we didn't rise at all.

02:57.140 --> 02:58.780
We didn't go up or down.

02:58.780 --> 03:01.220
So the slope here is 0.

03:01.220 --> 03:04.160
Or another way to think about
is this line has no inclination.

03:04.160 --> 03:08.620
It's a completely flat-- it's
a completely horizontal line.

03:08.620 --> 03:10.240
So this should make sense.

03:10.240 --> 03:12.230
This is a 0.

03:12.230 --> 03:14.570
The slope here is 0.

03:14.570 --> 03:16.090
And just to make
sure that this gels

03:16.090 --> 03:18.380
with all of these other
formulas that you might know--

03:18.380 --> 03:19.755
but I want to make
it very clear.

03:19.755 --> 03:21.860
These are all just
telling you rise over run

03:21.860 --> 03:25.750
or change in y over change in
x, a way to measure inclination.

03:25.750 --> 03:27.370
But let's just
apply them just so,

03:27.370 --> 03:28.970
hopefully, it all
makes sense to you.

03:28.970 --> 03:33.010
So we could also say slope is
change in y over change in x.

03:33.010 --> 03:37.170
If we take this to be
our start and if we

03:37.170 --> 03:39.520
take this to be our
end point, then we

03:39.520 --> 03:43.176
would call this over here x1.

03:43.176 --> 03:44.300
And then this is over here.

03:44.300 --> 03:45.550
This is y1.

03:45.550 --> 03:47.990
And then we would
call this x2 and we

03:47.990 --> 03:51.310
would call this y2, if
this is our start point

03:51.310 --> 03:52.860
and that is our end point.

03:52.860 --> 03:56.960
And so the slope here, the
change in y, y2 minus y1.

03:56.960 --> 04:02.880
So it's negative 1
minus negative 1,

04:02.880 --> 04:10.600
all of that over x2, negative
3, minus x1, minus 7.

04:10.600 --> 04:13.520
So the numerator, negative
1 minus negative 1,

04:13.520 --> 04:16.589
that's the same thing
as negative 1 plus 1.

04:16.589 --> 04:19.700
And our denominator
is negative 3 minus 7,

04:19.700 --> 04:22.200
which is negative 10.

04:22.200 --> 04:26.980
So once again, negative 1
plus 1 is 0 over negative 10.

04:26.980 --> 04:29.329
And this is still going to be 0.

04:29.329 --> 04:31.370
And the only reason why
we got a negative 10 here

04:31.370 --> 04:33.850
and a positive 10
there is because we

04:33.850 --> 04:36.110
swapped the starting
and the ending point.

04:36.110 --> 04:39.820
In this example right over here,
we took this as the start point

04:39.820 --> 04:42.870
and made this coordinate
over here as the end point.

04:42.870 --> 04:44.830
Over here, we
swapped them around.

04:44.830 --> 04:47.460
7, negative 1 was
our start point,

04:47.460 --> 04:49.880
and negative 3, negative
1 is our end point.

04:49.880 --> 04:54.170
So if we start over
here, our change in x

04:54.170 --> 04:56.070
is going to be negative 10.

04:56.070 --> 04:58.170
But our change in y is
still going to be 0.

04:58.170 --> 05:01.370
So regardless of how you do it,
the slope of this line is 0.

05:01.370 --> 05:03.866
It's a horizontal line.
